/**
  @page examples-index Examples and tutorials
  @brief Examples utilizing the Rodin library

  @tableofcontents

  @section examples-index-gallery Gallery

  For a visual overview of what Rodin can do, visit the @ref gallery "Gallery".

  @section examples-index-variational Variational methods and weak formulations

  The @ref Rodin::Variational "Variational" module is used when dealing with
  variational problems of the form:

  @f[
    \text{Find} \quad u \in U \quad \text{ s.t. } \quad \forall v \in V \quad
    a(u, v) = b(v) \ .
  @f]

  See the @ref guides-variational "variational formulations guide" for the
  mathematical background, the @ref notation-index "notation reference" for
  the symbols used, and the @ref guides-solvers "solvers guide" for choosing
  a solver.

  @section examples-index-pdes Solving PDEs

  %Rodin can be used to solve partial differential equations of many types. To
  start solving PDEs with Rodin, we recommend taking a look at the simplest
  example which is how to solve the Poisson equation.

  | Equation                                        | Link to example |
  | ----------------------------------------------- | --------------- |
  | @f$ - \Delta u = f @f$                          | @ref examples-pdes-poisson |
  | @f$ - \nabla \cdot \sigma (u ) = 0  @f$         | @ref examples-pdes-elasticity |
  | @f$ - \Delta u - k^2 u = f @f$                  | @ref examples-pdes-helmholtz |
  | @f$ -\nu \Delta \mathbf{u} + (\mathbf{u} \cdot \nabla)\mathbf{u} + \nabla p = \mathbf{f} @f$ | @ref examples-pdes-navierstokes |
  | @f$ - \Delta u = f @f$ (periodic)               | @ref examples-pdes-periodic |
  | @f$ - \Delta_\Gamma u + u = f @f$ (surface)     | @ref examples-pdes-surfacemesh |

  @section examples-index-io Input/Output

  Rodin supports several I/O formats for meshes, grid functions, and simulation
  data. The supported formats are @ref Rodin::IO::FileFormat "MFEM", MEDIT,
  and HDF5. The @ref Rodin::IO::XDMF "XDMF" format is recommended for
  exporting results for visualization in ParaView:

  - @subpage examples-io-index

  @section examples-index-geometry Mesh Geometry

  The @ref Rodin::Geometry "Geometry" module provides mesh creation,
  topological connectivity, and geometric operations:

  - @subpage examples-geometry-index

  @section examples-index-mmg Working with MMG

  The @ref Rodin::External::MMG "MMG" module has a wide variety of uses which
  ranges from optimization of the mesh, to implicit domain meshing. It provides
  many C++ wrappers to execute the functionality of the [Mmg
  Platform](https://www.mmgtools.org/):

  - @subpage examples-mmg-index

  @section examples-index-density_optimization Density optimization examples

  - @subpage examples-density_optimization-index

  @section examples-index-shapeoptimization Shape optimization examples

  - @subpage examples-shapeoptimization-index

  @section examples-index-solid Solid Mechanics

  The @ref Rodin::Solid "Solid" module provides hyperelastic constitutive
  laws and nonlinear integrators for large-deformation solid mechanics. The
  @ref Rodin::Elasticity "LinearElasticityIntegral" covers the small-deformation
  (linearized) case, while the Solid module handles finite-strain problems with
  constitutive laws such as NeoHookean, Saint-Venant-Kirchhoff, and
  Mooney-Rivlin:

  | Problem | Constitutive Law | Example |
  |---------|-----------------|---------|
  | Linear cantilever beam | @f$ \sigma = \lambda(\operatorname{tr}\varepsilon)I + 2\mu\varepsilon @f$ | @ref examples-pdes-elasticity |
  | Hyperelastic cantilever (NeoHookean, transient) | @f$ W = \frac{\mu}{2}(I_1-d) - \mu\ln J + \frac{\lambda}{2}(\ln J)^2 @f$ | `examples/Solid/CantileverBeam.cpp` |

  @section examples-index-eikonal Distance and Eikonal

  The @ref Rodin::Eikonal "Eikonal" module solves @f$ |\nabla u| = F(x) @f$
  via the Fast Marching Method, and the @ref Rodin::Distance "Distance" module
  wraps several distance-function computation strategies:

  | Method | Description | Example |
  |--------|-------------|---------|
  | FMM | Exact distance on unstructured meshes | `examples/Models/Eikonal/FMM.cpp` |
  | Spalding-Tucker | Boundary-layer wall distance | `examples/Models/Distance/SpaldingTucker.cpp` |

  @section examples-index-petsc PETSc Examples

  The @ref Rodin::PETSc "PETSc" module enables distributed solvers and
  multi-field block systems. Examples include:

  | Problem | Description | Example |
  |---------|-------------|---------|
  | Sequential Poisson | PETSc CG solver on local mesh | `examples/PETSc/PDEs/Seq_Poisson.cpp` |
  | MPI Poisson | Distributed mesh + PETSc KSP | `examples/PETSc/PDEs/MPI_Poisson.cpp` |
  | Sequential Stokes | Multi-field (velocity-pressure-Lagrange) | `examples/PETSc/PDEs/Seq_Stokes.cpp` |
  | Lid-driven cavity | Navier-Stokes with Picard iteration | `examples/PETSc/PDEs/Seq_LidDrivenCavity.cpp` |
  | Navier-Stokes | Nonlinear convection-diffusion | `examples/PETSc/PDEs/Seq_NavierStokes.cpp` |

  @see @ref guides-petsc "PETSc Guide", @ref guides-mpi "MPI Guide"

  @section examples-index-dg Discontinuous Galerkin

  The DG example solves the Poisson equation using the Symmetric Interior
  Penalty (SIP) method with DG-specific operators (`Jump`, `Average`,
  `FaceIntegral`, `InterfaceIntegral`):

  | Equation | Method | Example |
  |----------|--------|---------|
  | @f$ -\Delta u = f @f$ | SIP-DG | `examples/DG/Poisson.cpp` |

  @see @ref guides-variational "Variational Formulations Guide" for the DG section.

  */
